<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackageId>PicoLog</PackageId>
    <Description>High-performance structured logging with compile-time message templates and built-in sinks.</Description>
    <PackageTags>PicoHex;Logging;AOT</PackageTags>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="PicoLog.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(UseProjectReferences)' == 'true'">
    <ProjectReference Include="..\PicoLog.Abs\PicoLog.Abs.csproj" PrivateAssets="Build" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(UseProjectReferences)' != 'true'">
    <PackageReference Include="PicoLog.Abs" PrivateAssets="Build" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" Visible="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The other half of the "PicoLog carries no serializer" pin. AssemblySurfaceTests.
    PicoLog_ReferencesNoSerializer checks the ASSEMBLY's references, so it sees a serializer that
    is USED — including one that ships inside the shared framework (System.Text.Json lives in
    Microsoft.NETCore.App, so `using System.Text.Json` needs no PackageReference at all). What it
    cannot see is an UNUSED reference: Roslyn emits an AssemblyRef only for assemblies whose types
    are used, so a bare `<PackageReference Include="PicoJetson" />` leaves PicoLog.dll's metadata
    unchanged and that pin green (measured: its 194 tests still pass against exactly that build) —
    while every consumer of PicoLog still inherits the nuspec <dependency>. The project file is the
    only place that half is visible, and the dependency list is what ships.

    Both reference kinds are in scope, because both become <dependency> entries. NuGet builds that
    list from project.assets.json, so every ProjectReference declared here lands in the package
    whether or not the referenced project is packable: measured here, a plain
    `<ProjectReference Include="..\PicoLog.Gen\PicoLog.Gen.csproj" />` packed as
    `<dependency id="PicoLog.Gen" version="0.0.0-dev" exclude="Build,Analyzers" />` while this
    guard — which read only PackageReference — stayed green. That is the hole this target closes.

    (The PicoLog.Json shape needs two qualifiers. PicoLog.Json references PicoLog back, so restore
    stops at MSB4006 "circular dependency" before any guard runs. With that back-reference removed
    and PicoLog.dll already built, a no-build pack under the PackageReference-only guard still
    succeeded and listed `<dependency id="PicoLog.Json" version="0.0.0-dev" exclude="Build,Analyzers" />`
    plus the transitive `<dependency id="PicoJetson" version="2026.13.0" exclude="Build,Analyzers" />`.)

    The allow-list is exactly PicoLog.Abs, in both kinds: core's entire dependency list is
    PicoLog.Abs plus in-framework assemblies. Serialization is PicoLog.Json's job — the sinks that
    serialize (JsonLogSink, SeqSink) ship there, with PicoJetson. Both UseProjectReferences
    branches validate: project mode declares the one ProjectReference, package mode the one
    PackageReference, and an empty list is a pass.

    PrivateAssets is required on the allowed reference, and it is no loophole for a foreign one. On
    the ProjectReference, "Build" (what this file uses) keeps PicoLog.Abs's build assets out of the
    shipped dependency — PicoLog.Abs stays the one <dependency>, trimmed to
    `include="Runtime,Compile,Native,ContentFiles,Analyzers,BuildTransitive"`. "All" is different:
    it removes the <dependency> outright (measured the same way; it is how PicoLog.Abs hides its
    own PicoLog.Gen generator reference, whose nuspec group comes out empty), so on a dependency
    PicoLog.dll needs at run time it would ship an assembly consumers cannot load. A foreign id
    therefore fails whatever its PrivateAssets says: "all" hides the dependency while core keeps
    compiling against types no consumer receives.

    The two kinds are pinned differently on purpose: the PackageReference must be "Build" (the
    value this file sets, and PicoLog.Abs is the only dependency that build declares, so drift
    there is a packaging change), while the ProjectReference only has to be non-empty and free of
    "all" — a different flow filter there is a build-graph decision, and any value other than
    "all" keeps PicoLog.Abs in the nuspec.

    CASE DOES NOT MATTER on either comparison, and that is deliberate, not an accident of
    MSBuild's `!=`. Measured twice here. (a) MSBuild's own string comparison is case-insensitive:
    in a .proj, `Condition="'build' != 'Build'"` fires no error — so the earlier check already
    accepted `PrivateAssets="build"`. (b) NuGet agrees: packing this file with
    `PrivateAssets="build"` on the ProjectReference produced a dependency group identical (empty
    diff) to the `"Build"` one — the single `<dependency id="PicoLog.Abs" ... include=`
    `"Runtime,Compile,Native,ContentFiles,Analyzers,BuildTransitive" />`. Rejecting `build` would
    fail a package identical to the accepted one, so the check stays case-insensitive; it is now
    an explicit OrdinalIgnoreCase comparison, so the behaviour is stated rather than inherited
    from a comparison operator's default, and the messages say so.

    The allow-list is matched on the PATH, not the file name. It used to test
    `GetFileNameWithoutExtension(...) == 'PicoLog.Abs'`, which accepted ANY PicoLog.Abs.csproj
    anywhere in the tree (a different project whose id is whatever it declares) while the message
    still claimed "exactly one project". The expected path is ..\PicoLog.Abs\PicoLog.Abs.csproj
    relative to this file; both sides go through NormalizePath and are compared case-insensitively,
    so the verdict does not flip between the Windows and the Linux/macOS CI legs. It does not
    survive a rename of the referenced project, which is intended: the dependency id and the
    packaging flow come from that project.

    Scope: the two reference kinds declared in THIS file (%(DefiningProjectFullPath)); references
    injected by Directory.Build.props (SourceLink, ILCompiler, ILLink) are out of it.
    NOT covered: those, <NuspecFile>, a dependency's buildTransitive targets, <Reference> to a vendored DLL.

    Timing: Build and GenerateNuspec. Restore never runs it, so a first-time restore is unaffected.

    GenerateNuspec, not Pack: MSBuild runs a target's DependsOnTargets before any target hooked
    via BeforeTargets, and Pack depends on GenerateNuspec (which itself depends on Build unless
    NoBuild is set). Hooking Pack therefore fired the guard AFTER the package had been written.
    Measured on this worktree with the offending reference present: a no-build pack
    (`dotnet pack -p:NoBuild=true`) printed "Successfully created package
    ...PicoLog.0.0.0-dev.nupkg" ABOVE the guard error, and that .nupkg really listed
    `<dependency id="PicoJetson" version="2026.13.0" exclude="Build,Analyzers" />`; the exit
    code was 1, so CI still failed and no push step ran, but a pipeline that collects
    bin/**/*.nupkg regardless of exit status would have carried the package. GenerateNuspec is
    the last target that runs before the file is written, so failing there leaves no .nupkg on
    disk. Build is kept so a plain `build` fails too, and because a plain `pack` builds first: the
    guard then fires at Build, before nuspec generation is even reached.
  -->
  <Target Name="PicoLogCoreDeclaresNoSerializer" BeforeTargets="Build;GenerateNuspec">
    <PropertyGroup>
      <_PicoLogCoreAllowedProjectPath>$([MSBuild]::NormalizePath('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)', '..', 'PicoLog.Abs', 'PicoLog.Abs.csproj').ToLowerInvariant())</_PicoLogCoreAllowedProjectPath>
    </PropertyGroup>
    <ItemGroup>
      <!--
        Only references DECLARED IN THIS FILE: the well-known DefiningProjectFullPath metadata is
        matched against the project MSBuild was invoked on, so Directory.Build.props additions
        (SourceLink, the SDK's ILCompiler / ILLink packages) are invisible to the guard.
      -->
      <_PicoLogCoreOwnPackageReference
        Include="@(PackageReference)"
        Condition="'%(PackageReference.DefiningProjectFullPath)' == '$(MSBuildProjectFullPath)'"
      />
      <_PicoLogCoreOwnProjectReference
        Include="@(ProjectReference)"
        Condition="'%(ProjectReference.DefiningProjectFullPath)' == '$(MSBuildProjectFullPath)'"
      />
      <!-- The one allowed project, by PATH. A ProjectReference's Identity is a path; the file
           name alone accepted any PicoLog.Abs.csproj anywhere in the tree. Both sides are
           normalized (either separator, "." and ".." included) and lowercased, so they compare
           with `==` and keep the "@(x) is empty" idiom of the error conditions below. -->
      <_PicoLogCoreAllowedProjectReference
        Include="@(_PicoLogCoreOwnProjectReference)"
        Condition="$([MSBuild]::NormalizePath('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)', '%(Identity)').ToLowerInvariant()) == '$(_PicoLogCoreAllowedProjectPath)'"
      />
      <_PicoLogCoreForeignPackageReference
        Include="@(_PicoLogCoreOwnPackageReference)"
        Exclude="PicoLog.Abs"
      />
      <_PicoLogCoreForeignProjectReference
        Include="@(_PicoLogCoreOwnProjectReference)"
        Exclude="@(_PicoLogCoreAllowedProjectReference)"
      />
      <!-- The allowed id, with the PrivateAssets that keeps its build assets out of the shipped
           dependency. "all" is caught explicitly: it would drop the dependency instead. -->
      <_PicoLogCoreUnpinnedPackageReference
        Include="@(_PicoLogCoreOwnPackageReference)"
        Condition="'%(Identity)' == 'PicoLog.Abs' and !$([System.String]::Equals('%(_PicoLogCoreOwnPackageReference.PrivateAssets)', 'Build', System.StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))"
      />
      <_PicoLogCoreUnpinnedProjectReference
        Include="@(_PicoLogCoreOwnProjectReference)"
        Condition="$([MSBuild]::NormalizePath('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)', '%(Identity)').ToLowerInvariant()) == '$(_PicoLogCoreAllowedProjectPath)' and ('%(_PicoLogCoreOwnProjectReference.PrivateAssets)' == '' or $([System.String]::Copy('%(_PicoLogCoreOwnProjectReference.PrivateAssets)').ToLowerInvariant().Contains('all')))"
      />
    </ItemGroup>
    <Error
      Condition="'@(_PicoLogCoreForeignPackageReference)' != ''"
      Text="PicoLog (core) may declare exactly one package dependency: PicoLog.Abs. Offending PackageReference(s): @(_PicoLogCoreForeignPackageReference, ', ').&#10;&#10;Serialization is PicoLog.Json's job: the sinks that serialize (JsonLogSink, SeqSink) ship in that package, together with PicoJetson. Core's entire dependency list is PicoLog.Abs plus in-framework assemblies — a package declared here is inherited by every consumer of PicoLog. If a sink needs it, that sink belongs in PicoLog.Json.&#10;&#10;This guard covers the UNUSED reference, which leaves PicoLog.dll's metadata untouched; a serializer that is actually USED additionally trips AssemblySurfaceTests.PicoLog_ReferencesNoSerializer."
    />
    <Error
      Condition="'@(_PicoLogCoreForeignProjectReference)' != ''"
      Text="PicoLog (core) may reference exactly one project: PicoLog.Abs, and it must be the one this file declares (..\PicoLog.Abs\PicoLog.Abs.csproj). Offending ProjectReference(s): @(_PicoLogCoreForeignProjectReference->'%(Identity) [PrivateAssets=%(PrivateAssets)]', ', ').&#10;&#10;The allow-list is that exact project path, not the file name PicoLog.Abs.csproj: any other project that happens to be called PicoLog.Abs.csproj is a different project with its own id and packaging flow, and the dependency a ProjectReference here produces comes from the referenced project.&#10;&#10;A ProjectReference declared in this file becomes a nuspec dependency of the packed PicoLog — one that every consumer of PicoLog then inherits. NuGet builds that list from project.assets.json, so a reference to a non-packable project is included too (measured here: a plain reference to PicoLog.Gen packed as a PicoLog.Gen dependency), and PrivateAssets=&quot;all&quot; only hides the dependency while core keeps compiling against project types that no consumer receives. Serialization is PicoLog.Json's job: the sinks that serialize (JsonLogSink, SeqSink) ship in that package, together with PicoJetson. If a sink needs it, that sink belongs in PicoLog.Json.&#10;&#10;This guard covers the UNUSED reference, which leaves PicoLog.dll's metadata untouched; a serializer that is actually USED additionally trips AssemblySurfaceTests.PicoLog_ReferencesNoSerializer."
    />
    <Error
      Condition="'@(_PicoLogCoreUnpinnedPackageReference)' != ''"
      Text="PicoLog (core) may declare exactly one package dependency: PicoLog.Abs, and it must carry PrivateAssets=&quot;Build&quot;. Case does not matter: MSBuild and NuGet both treat &quot;build&quot; and &quot;Build&quot; identically (measured — packing with PrivateAssets=&quot;build&quot; yields the same nuspec dependency group as &quot;Build&quot;), so &quot;build&quot; and &quot;BUILD&quot; pass. What fails is an empty PrivateAssets, &quot;all&quot;, or any other value: this dependency is pinned to &quot;Build&quot; and nothing wider, so even the explicit flow list Build,BuildTransitive,BuildMultitargeting is rejected as drift. Offending PackageReference(s): @(_PicoLogCoreUnpinnedPackageReference, ', ').&#10;&#10;PrivateAssets=&quot;Build&quot; is what keeps PicoLog.Abs's build assets out of the shipped dependency; PrivateAssets=&quot;all&quot; would remove the dependency itself, and PicoLog.dll cannot be used without PicoLog.Abs.&#10;&#10;This guard covers the UNUSED reference, which leaves PicoLog.dll's metadata untouched; a serializer that is actually USED additionally trips AssemblySurfaceTests.PicoLog_ReferencesNoSerializer."
    />
    <Error
      Condition="'@(_PicoLogCoreUnpinnedProjectReference)' != ''"
      Text="PicoLog (core)'s one allowed ProjectReference — the project this file names, ..\PicoLog.Abs\PicoLog.Abs.csproj — must carry PrivateAssets and must not include &quot;all&quot;. Offending ProjectReference(s): @(_PicoLogCoreUnpinnedProjectReference->'%(Identity) [PrivateAssets=%(PrivateAssets)]', ', ').&#10;&#10;PrivateAssets=&quot;Build&quot; (what this file uses) is what keeps PicoLog.Abs's build assets out of the shipped dependency. Case does not matter here either: &quot;build&quot; and &quot;Build&quot; are the same to MSBuild and NuGet (measured). Without it the reference flows its assets to every consumer project; with &quot;all&quot; the dependency disappears from the nuspec altogether — and PicoLog.dll needs PicoLog.Abs at compile and run time, so consumers would receive an assembly they cannot load.&#10;&#10;This guard covers the UNUSED reference, which leaves PicoLog.dll's metadata untouched; a serializer that is actually USED additionally trips AssemblySurfaceTests.PicoLog_ReferencesNoSerializer."
    />
  </Target>
</Project>
